        "sensor_height_cm": 900,
        "note": "Doc 16 section 9 item 2's six sections, and cut X.2's numbers for them. The front band is the torso and the two arms it carries its weapons in, with the head a rare fifth; the legs are struck from the flanks and from behind, which is what the side and rear bands weight them for. Eleven metres tall and 5.2 m across the shoulders is a bigger target than the line tank from ahead (350 cm) and a thinner one from the flank (700 cm), so a biped wants to be side on and a tank does not. The sight sits 9 m up on the head. The legged mode walks slower than the tank drives, accelerates and stops harder, twists at 45 degrees a second where a hull slews at 30, and backs at 40 per cent. One leg gone is slow(50), which is doc 16 section 3.3's track consequence read for a walker; both gone is disable_motion, authored on each leg as the pair so the order they are lost in does not matter."
